## Answer

**The statement is FALSE.**

**Interpretation.** "The identity operator can be approximated by finite range operators" is the standard formulation of the *approximation property*: for every compact set $K \subseteq Y$ and every $\varepsilon > 0$, there exists a finite-rank operator $T:Y\to Y$ with $\|Tx-x\|\le \varepsilon$ for all $x\in K$ (i.e. the identity lies in the closure of the finite-rank operators in the topology of uniform convergence on compact sets). Under this (the standard) reading the claim is false.

**Counterexample.** Let $\Gamma$ be an uncountable set and let
$$Y=\ell^{2}(\Gamma)=\Bigl\{x:\Gamma\to\mathbb{C}\;\Big|\;\sum_{\gamma\in\Gamma}|x(\gamma)|^{2}<\infty\Bigr\}.$$
This is a Hilbert space with inner product $\langle x,y\rangle=\sum_{\gamma}x(\gamma)\overline{y(\gamma)}$.

*Non-separability.* The family $\{e_{\gamma}:\gamma\in\Gamma\}$ of standard unit vectors is an orthonormal system of cardinality $|\Gamma|$, which is uncountable. A Hilbert space is separable iff it admits a countable orthonormal basis iff its Hilbert dimension is countable. Since $\ell^{2}(\Gamma)$ has uncountable Hilbert dimension, it is **not separable**.

*Approximation property.* Let $K\subseteq Y$ be compact and $\varepsilon>0$. Compactness gives total boundedness, so there exist $x_{1},\dots,x_{n}\in K$ with $K\subseteq\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}B(x_{i},\varepsilon/2)$. Set $M=\operatorname{span}\{x_{1},\dots,x_{n}\}$, a finite-dimensional subspace, and let $P:Y\to M$ be the orthogonal projection, a finite-rank operator with $\|P\|=1$. For any $x\in K$ pick $x_{i}$ with $\|x-x_{i}\|<\varepsilon/2$. Since $x_{i}\in M$, $Px_{i}=x_{i}$, and
$$\|Px-x\|\le\|P(x-x_{i})\|+\|Px_{i}-x_{i}\|+\|x_{i}-x\|\le\|P\|\,\|x-x_{i}\|+0+\|x-x_{i}\|<2\cdot\frac{\varepsilon}{2}=\varepsilon.$$
Thus for every compact $K$ and $\varepsilon>0$ there is a finite-rank operator $P$ with $\|Px-x\|<\varepsilon$ on $K$: the identity is approximable (uniformly on compact sets) by finite-rank operators.

**Conclusion.** $Y=\ell^{2}(\Gamma)$ (uncountable $\Gamma$) is a normed space in which the identity operator can be approximated by finite-range operators, yet $Y$ is not separable. Therefore the implication

> "identity approximable by finite-range operators $\Rightarrow$ $Y$ separable"

is **false**. (The source of the error is conflating the approximation property with the stronger property of having a Schauder basis, the latter of which *does* force separability.)

